; ======================================================================
; anvilqBootPayload.unoq - the payload Anvil BOOTS on the Arduino UNO Q,
; for the silicon proof of the bootloader.
;
; This is not a demonstration of anything the Q can do. It is the other
; half of a two-part measurement, and its whole job is to leave EVIDENCE
; that it ran which cannot be confused with anything else:
;
;   * it writes a signature word to a fixed address in DRAM, and
;   * it returns a known value to the monitor that entered it.
;
; Anvil is then asked to dump that address, and the transcript on the ESP
; carries the answer off the board. Without a payload that leaves a mark,
; "the monitor said it entered it" is the monitor's own word for it - and
; the entire point of this exercise is that the monitor's word is what is
; under test.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship (rule 19).
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW IT IS BUILT, AND WHY EVERY FLAG IS THERE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile ArduinoQ/Examples/Diagnostics/anvilqBootPayload.unoq -t unoq \
;        --load-addr 0x64000000 --bss-addr 0x64100000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x64F00000 --entry-returns \
;        -o _work/APP.IMG
;
; and the file that is actually staged on the board is the one the compiler
; writes BESIDE that: _work/APP.IMG.pmf, the payload container. It is the
; container that carries the load address, the entry point, the BSS
; extent and the SHA-256, which is why `boot APP.PMF` needs no address.
;
;   --load-addr 0x64000000  inside the Q's confirmed contiguous free DRAM
;                           block (0x63900000..0x7D9FFFFF, read off the
;                           running board from /proc/iomem), inside the
;                           low payload window (0x40000000..0xFFFFFFFF),
;                           and clear of the nominal monitor windows at
;                           0x63900000..0x63AFFFFF that HitsMonitor()
;                           refuses. All three constraints come from
;                           ArduinoQ/Board/memmap_q.unoq.
;   --bss-addr  0x64100000  a megabyte above the image, in the same free
;                           block. It is stated EXPLICITLY rather than
;                           left to the compiler's default so that the
;                           container's BSS extent is a number a reader
;                           can check against the memory map instead of
;                           one that moves when the program grows.
;   --stack-addr 0x64F00000 INSIDE the free block, above the BSS, growing
;                           down towards it with fourteen megabytes of
;                           room. This payload uses a handful of frames.
;
;                           IT WAS 0x63800000 AND THAT HUNG THE BOARD.
;                           The reasoning was "below the image, so the
;                           stack grows away from it" - true, and beside
;                           the point: 0x63800000 is also below
;                           0x63900000, where the confirmed free block
;                           STARTS. Everything under that is Qualcomm
;                           TZ/firmware/modem reserved aperture, read off
;                           the running board from /proc/iomem.
;
;                           AND THE STACK SWITCH HAPPENS EVEN UNDER
;                           --entry-returns, which is what makes it fatal
;                           rather than merely untidy.
;                           EmitCore_A64.pbi:335-341 says so in as many
;                           words - "THE STACK SWITCH IS STILL
;                           UNCONDITIONAL, and that is deliberate" - and
;                           then pushes the loader's x30 and SP onto it.
;                           So the first thing this payload did on silicon
;                           was write sixteen bytes into a reserved
;                           aperture, and the board stopped answering.
;
;                           THE LESSON, worth more than the fix: on this
;                           board --stack-addr is not a hint. A payload
;                           that RETURNS still moves SP, so a stack
;                           address outside real DRAM is exactly as fatal
;                           as a load address outside it - and unlike the
;                           load address, nothing in the container
;                           describes it and nothing in Anvil can check
;                           it.
;   --entry-returns         THE LOAD-BEARING ONE. It makes _start end in a
;                           real epilogue, so this program RETURNS to
;                           Anvil instead of spinning for ever. That is
;                           what lets the same board run continue: Anvil
;                           gets its prompt back, the staged script asks
;                           it to dump the signature address, and the
;                           transcript carries the evidence off the board.
;                           Without it the session would end here and the
;                           only thing provable would be that the board
;                           stopped answering.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BENCH RECIPE, AND WHY IT IS IN THIS ORDER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PROVE THE PROMPT-DRIVEN PATH BEFORE ARMING THE AUTOMATIC ONE. Anvil
; flushes its transcript to the ESP after EVERY command, so a session
; driven from \EFInvilnvil.in leaves evidence up to the last command
; that completed - even if the next one wedges the board. The automatic
; boot happens BEFORE the prompt exists, so if it wedges there is no
; transcript at all and nothing to read afterwards. That is precisely
; what happened on the first attempt here: the board went quiet and the
; only thing recoverable was that it had gone quiet.
;
; So stage this in anvil.in first, with NO boot.file set:
;
;     load APP.PMF 64800000
;     memory 64800000 20
;     boot mem 64800000
;     memory 64200000 20
;     boot status
;     exit
;
; `load` proves the file seam reaches the ESP. The first `memory` proves
; the container arrived and shows its header. `boot mem` runs the whole
; verify-place-enter path with a transcript already on disk behind it.
; The second `memory` is the payload's signature, read back.
;
; ONLY THEN arm the automatic path - setenv boot.file APP.PMF, saveenv -
; and reboot to watch the countdown do the same thing by itself.
;
; A HANG HERE COSTS A HUMAN. Debian is the default boot entry and the
; systemd-boot one-shot is consumed before the image is launched, so a
; power cycle always returns the board - but somebody has to walk over
; and do it. That is the whole reason for the ordering above.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IT PRINTS NOTHING, AND THAT IS NOT A LIMITATION OF THIS FILE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; There is no UART on this bench that anybody can read - the GENI SE4 is
; 1.8 V - and the console Anvil uses is a UEFI protocol reached through
; the SystemTable pointer the firmware handed to ANVIL, not to this
; program. A payload entered by RunAt() gets no arguments at all: the Q's
; RunAt calls it with five zeroes.
;
; So this payload writes to memory, which needs nothing from anybody, and
; Anvil reads that memory back afterwards. Passing the SystemTable through
; to a payload would be a real and useful thing to build - it is how a
; booted program on this board would ever say anything for itself - and it
; is deliberately not built here, because inventing a calling convention
; for payloads is a design decision and not a test fixture.
; ======================================================================

XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/memmap_q.unoq"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE EVIDENCE. Both numbers are stated here, in the deploy script that
;  reads them back, and in the report - three places, so a transcript can
;  be checked without running anything.
;
;  #QBP_MARK is where the signature goes. It is inside the low payload
;  window and inside the confirmed free block, a megabyte clear of this
;  program's own BSS at 0x64100000, so nothing this program does can
;  write it by accident.
;
;  #QBP_SIG is a value that appears nowhere else in this system: not a
;  zero, not an all-ones, not an address, not a length, and not a value
;  any of Anvil's own structures hold. If it is at that address, this
;  program put it there.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#QBP_MARK = $64200000          ; where the signature is written
#QBP_SIG  = $B007ED00600D      ; and what is written there
#QBP_RC   = $600DB007          ; and what comes back in x0

Procedure.i Main()
  ; The signature. One 64-bit store, so it either happened or it did not -
  ; there is no half-written state to misread.
  PokeI(#QBP_MARK, #QBP_SIG)

  ; A SECOND WORD, eight bytes on, holding the address this program
  ; believes it is running at. It is not needed to prove the boot
  ; happened, and it is here because it is the cheapest possible answer
  ; to the question that follows: "was it placed where the container said
  ; it would be?" If the monitor placed the image somewhere else, this
  ; program would still have run - flat A64 code with no absolute
  ; references mostly does - and this word is what would disagree.
  PokeI(#QBP_MARK + 8, #QBP_MARK)

  ; The value the monitor sees come back in x0. Distinct from the
  ; signature so that "the payload ran" and "the payload returned
  ; normally" are two separate observations and not one.
  ProcedureReturn #QBP_RC
EndProcedure

Main()
